From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TinyYana <yanasakuranight@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:00:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] do not drain another world's pending chunk full-status
 updates

ChunkHolderManager#processTicketUpdates ends with

    if (isTickThread) { ret |= this.processPendingFullUpdate(); }

and processPendingFullUpdate resolves the region this thread is ticking through
getCurrentRegionData(), which asserts that region's world against this
manager's and throws "World check failed" when they differ.

isTickThread is the wrong question. The drain needs a tick thread OF THIS
WORLD, not any tick thread. The line immediately above already asks the right
one: addChangedStatuses compares
world.regioniser.getRegionAtUnsynchronised(...) == thisRegion and routes
everything else through scheduleChunkTaskEventually.

Nothing in the platform reaches this for a foreign world, so upstream never
trips it. A cross-world chunk load does. ChunkTaskScheduler#syncLoadNonFull
adds a NON_FULL_CHUNK_LOAD ticket, calls processTicketUpdates on the target
world and then waits; called from a region thread of another world it threw
between adding that ticket and removing it, and syncLoadNonFull has no finally.
NON_FULL_CHUNK_LOAD is registered with no timeout, so the ticket was permanent:
one pinned chunk holder, and through onChunkHolderCreate one region section in
the target world, leaked per distinct chunk ever read that way.

The leak is what hid it. The chunk stayed resident, so the second read of the
same block succeeded - which is why a probe that read a spawn chunk, or read
twice, reported the feature working.

Measured on the A/B pair, a player's own region thread reading nether2 at
(200000, 200000):

  before   first read   THREW  + World check failed: expected world:
                               minecraft:nether2, region world: minecraft:overworld
           second read  AIR    isChunkLoaded=true
           20s later    AIR    isChunkLoaded=true      <- never released

Skipping is correct rather than merely quieter. The queue being drained belongs
to the region this thread is ticking, i.e. to the other world, and applying it
through this manager is exactly what the assertion exists to prevent. The
target world's own regions drain their own queues when they tick, so nothing is
dropped and nothing is deferred that would not have been deferred anyway.

No ownership check is weakened: the assertion still fires for anything that
genuinely reaches it with mismatched state. What changes is that a caller with
no business processing that queue no longer walks into it.

Kill switch: compat-config.foreign-world-ticket-updates=false.

diff --git a/ca/spottedleaf/moonrise/patches/chunk_system/scheduling/ChunkHolderManager.java b/ca/spottedleaf/moonrise/patches/chunk_system/scheduling/ChunkHolderManager.java
index 8b06d0c893e07c969d35c4d7528927552c27b48b..0742a1cb65499df06ba0f319e4eab18a47814413 100644
--- a/ca/spottedleaf/moonrise/patches/chunk_system/scheduling/ChunkHolderManager.java
+++ b/ca/spottedleaf/moonrise/patches/chunk_system/scheduling/ChunkHolderManager.java
@@ -1596,7 +1596,14 @@ public final class ChunkHolderManager {
             }
         }
 
-        if (isTickThread) {
+        // Lecithin start - a tick thread of THIS world, not any tick thread.
+        // processPendingFullUpdate drains the pending queue of the region this thread is ticking, and
+        // getCurrentRegionData asserts that region's world against this manager's. Reached for a
+        // foreign world - which a cross-world chunk load does - it throws after syncLoadNonFull has
+        // added its ticket and before it removes it, leaking that ticket permanently. The line above
+        // this one already asks the right question; see LecithinForeignWorldTicketUpdates.
+        if (isTickThread && fun.bm.lecithin.compat.LecithinForeignWorldTicketUpdates.mayProcessPendingFullUpdate(this.world)) {
+        // Lecithin end
             ret |= this.processPendingFullUpdate();
         }
 
